\begin{documenttitle}
Fluorinated Polyimides for Optical Applications
\end{documenttitle}

Tohru Matsuura

NTT Science and Core Technology Laboratory Group, Nippon Telegraph and Telephone Corporation, 3-9-11, Midori-cho, Musashino-shi, Tokyo 180, Japan

Fluorinated polyimides are synthesized for optical application. They have good thermal stability, low water absorption, high optical transparency in visible and near-infrared regions, and refravtive index controllability. Film optical waveguide is fabricated using the fluorinated co-polyimides. This optical waveguide has single-mode and low optical loss of less than 0.3 dB/cm at the telecommunication wavelength of 1.3 um. This waveguide has high thermal and environmental stability.

Keywords: polyimide, fluarination, co-polymerization, optical transparency, thermal stability, refractive index control, optical waveguide

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

Optical telecommunications transmitting a very large amount of information by light signals have recently been rapidly replacing conventional electrical telecommunications. In the near future, opto-electronic integrated circuits (OE-IC) and opto-electronic multichip modules (OE-MCM) will be produced. Materials with high thermal stability will thus become very important in b providing compatibility with conventional IC fabrication processes and in ensuring device reliability.P Polyimides have excellent thermal −√ stability so they are often used as electronic materials. St. Clair et al. have reported that the fluorinated polyimides with -C(CF₃)2- and -SO2- groups have a higher optical transparency compared with non-fluorinated polyimides.[1] Furuya et al. have introduced polyimide as an optical interconnect material for the first time.[2] Reuter et al. have applied polyimides to optical interconnects, and have evaluated the fluorinated polyimides as optical waveguide materials.[3] Sullivan has fabricated high-density optical waveguide components using polyimides to √i implement optical interconnects.[4] Beuhler et al. have achieved to fabricate low loss buried

optical waveguide using a fluorinated polyimide with a photo-crosslinking group.[5]

We have synthesized fluorinated polyimides with high optical transparency for optical telecommunications.[6, 7] This paper describes the properties of polyimides and their optical applications.

\begin{title}
2. Primary Requirements for Optical Applications
\end{title}

With the progress in optical communications, there has been a corresponding demand for the use of polyimides in optical components. Table 1 summarizes the primary requirements for optical communication n materials and the approach used in this work. Thermal stability above 300°C is required for compatibility with conventional IC fabrication. Polyimides have sufficient thermal stability in addition to easy processability for this use.

Optical materials are required for high optical transparency at adequate wavelength for the components. The near-infrared wavelengths of 1.3 and 1.5 μm are needed for optical communications

