(long distance telecommunication). Most organic polymers, including conventional √i polyimides, have light absorption based on the vibration of chemical bonds. 1 Indeed, the harmonics of the vibration of carbon-hydrogen (C-H) bonds and oxygen-hydrogen (O-H) bonds are located at the near-infrared wavelength. Introducing fluorine instead of hydrogen reduces the light absorption and improves optical , transparency in polyimides.[8] On the other hand, the visible wavelengths of 0.63 and 0.85 μm are also needed for local area networks (LANs). Light absorption at the visible wavelengths can be explained by electronic c charge transfer.[9] Optical transparency increases with decreasing molecular interaction and with shorter intra- molecular π-conjugation. Introducing fluorine into polyimides is an effective way. Coloration of polyimides and their electronic charge transfer has been discussed elsewhere.[10]

Having control of the refractive index is fundamental and important in using optical materials as well as optical transparency. The  refractive index must be precisely controlled because it is essential for optical components such as single-mode optical waveguides. This control can be achieved by copolymerization of low and high refractive index polyimides.

Low stress is important for the optical components, because a high registration rate in device fabrication and good device reliability are achieved. A cause of stress is the heat cycle in the device fabrication of the different materials which have different coefficients of thermal expansion (CTEs). The stress produced by this CTE

difference causes cracking, peeling, and bending.[11] Furthermore, it also changes the refractive index of materials. Thermal expansion control is also achieved by copolymerization of low and high thermal expansion polyimides.[6]

Long-term durability is one of the basic specifications for optical components, and polyimides have excellent physical, chemical, and mechanical stability. In practice, optical polymers for such as optical waveguides must retain their optical properties and must be reliable in humid environments. Introducing fluorine into polyimides is the most effective way of reducing the water absorption.[12]

Organic materials with good flexibility is suitable for stability against physical impact and for high density packaging. Furthermore, their good processability and low price enable us to make light-weight components at a low fabrication cost.

\begin{title}
3. Properties of Fluorinated Polyimides
\end{title}

\begin{title}
3.1. Fundamental Properties
\end{title}

