TFDB[13] is suitable for rigid-rod fluorinated polyimides. The polyimides synthesized from 6FDA have a bent structure, and this causes a slight decrease in glass transition temperature (Tg). Good thermal stability for high-fluorine-content polyimides is maintained by introducing rigid-rod TFDB units.

Table 2 shows the characteristics of the high- fluorine-content 6FDA/TFDB polyimide, low- fluorine-content PMDA/TFDB polyimide, and KAPTON (non-fluorinated PMDAODA type polyimide, du Pont). Both 6FDA/TFDB and PMDA/TFDB polyimides have Tgs above 300C, and have high thermal stability.

The water absorptions of high-fluorine-content 6FDA/TFDB and low-fluorine-content PMDA/TFDB are very small at 0.2% and 0.7%, because of the waterproofing effect of fluorine atoms. This is smaller than that of PMDA/ODA at 1.3%.

The CTE of 6FDA/TFDB is a little higher than that of PMDA/ODA, because 6FDA/TFDB have high CTEs, that is, their main chains have bent hexafluoroisopropylidene units. On the other hand, the PMDA/TFDB has a rather low fluorine content of 23.0%; however, its CTE is extremely low at -5 x 10C.. This low expansion fluorinated polyimide, PMDA/TFDB is unique and very useful as a low thermal expansion component in optical materials.

\begin{title}
3.2. Optical Loss in Visible Region
\end{title}

Introducing fluorine into polyimides is an effective way of improving their optical transparency. The fluorinated polyimides 6FDA/TFDB and PMDA/TFDB and their copolyimides have a high optical transparency at visible wavelengths compared with conventional polyimides.

The optical losses of several polyimides are shown in Fig. 2. 6FDA/TFDB has the lowest optical loss of 0.7 dB/cm of all polyimides. Compared with 6FDA/TFDB, either PMDA/TFDB prepared with PMDA instead of 6FDA or 6FDA/DMDB prepared with DMDB instead of TFDB has a higher optical loss of about 5 dB/cm, and PMDA/DMDB prepared with non-fluorinated PMDA and DMDB has the highest optical loss of 36 dB/cm. The optical loss of polyimide decreases when -C(CF₃)2- groups are introduced into the polyimide main chain, or when -CF₃ groups are introduced into the biphenyl group as the side chain.

\begin{title}
3.3. Optical Loss in Near-infrared Region
\end{title}

