\begin{title}
3.4.Refractive index Control
\end{title}

Controlling the properties of polyimides is very important in applying them to optical materials. The refractive index is controlled by the composition ratio of the two fluorinated polyimides, 6FDA/TFDB and PMDA/TFDB. The refractive index of the co-polyimide can be precisely controlled by varying the 6FDA/TFDB ratio. We used a 6FDA/TFDB-rich co-polyimide, and introduced a small amount of PMDA/TFDB for refractive index control. The fluorinated copolyimides are all more transparent than the typical non-fluorinated polyimides such as PMDA/ODA. The in-plane refractive index (nre) and out-of-plane refractive index (nrm) at 1.3 μm of 6FDA/TFDB, PMDA/TFDB， and their copolyimides cured at 350°C on a silicon substrate are shown in Fig. 4. The nre decreases with increasing 6FDA/TFDB content, and can be controlled precisely between 1.523 and 1.614 by changing the 6FDA/TFDB content. In the high 6FDA/TFDB content region, the slopes of the curves are gentle, and more accurate refractive index control is achieved. The nre decreases with increasing 6FDA/TFDB content, but nrm has a maximum at a 6FDA/TFDB content of 60 mol%. The nrm can also be controlled between 1.514 and 1.521 by changing the 6FDA/TFDB content from 60 to 100 mol%. The nre is always higher than nrm at the same 6FDA/TFDB content.

