optical waveguides were fabricated using the fluorinated copolyimides with a high optical transparency and refractive index controllability. The high thermal stability of these waveguide materials should provide OE-IC fabrication processes and device reliability against heat- cycling.

\begin{title}
4.1. Waveguide Fabrication.
\end{title}

Optical film waveguides were were fabricated from the polyimides by using a combination of spin-casting, conventional photolithographic patterning, and reactive ion etching (RIE). An under-cladding layer and a core layer of the polyimides were formed on a temporary substrate. The core ridge was then fabricated by photo- lithographic patterning and RIE using oxygen. An over-cladding layer was formed over the core ridge.I b√ Finally, the substrate was removed from the polyimide waveguide. Figure 5 shows cross- sectional micrographs of the film waveguide. The square cores (8 x 8 μm) are completely embedded in the cladding layer. The refractive index difference between the core and cladding is about 0.4%, as measured from the interference micrograph.

Single-mode operation of the waveguides is identified from near-field mode patterns (NFPs). The light intensity of the NFP has a Gaussian distribution with the strongest intensity located at the core center. This waveguide shows single- mode behavior at 1.3 m.

\begin{title}
4.2. Optical Loss.
\end{title}

Figure 6 shows the dependence of optical loss, including connection loss for TE polarization in the waveguide. This spectrum is mostly similar to those of polyimide materials. The optical loss for light absorption at near-infrared wavelengths is mainly due to the harmonics and the couplings of stretching (v) and deformation(δ) vibrations at chemical bonds such as C-H and O-H bonds.

