\begin{title}
4.4. Bending Loss.
\end{title}

This film waveguide is free-standing and has good flexibility. Figure 8 shows the optical loss of the waveguide after bending with various curvature radii. The low optical loss and single- mode behavior were maintained when the curvature radius was over 20 mm. At smaller curvature radii, the light is not fully confined to the core, so optical loss increases with decreasing radius.

\begin{title}
4.5.Birefringence of Film Waveguide.
\end{title}

One of the features of this film waveguide is its very low in-planelout-of-plane birefringence. We calculated the birefringence of the film waveguide to be a very low of 9 x 10-5 by the Senarmont method.

This single-mode free-standing film waveguide has high thermal stability and good flexibility because it uses fluorinated polyimides as heat- resistant polymeric material.

\begin{title}
5. Conclusion
\end{title}

Fluorinated polyimides with high thermal stability were synthesized for optical applications. Introducing fluorine into polyimides at adequate structure achieves a high optical transparency in the visible and near-infrared wavelengths region, low water absorption, and low refractive index. The refractive index can be precisely controlled by copolymerization of the high fluorine content 6FDA/TFDB polyimide and the low fluorine content PMDA/TFDB polyimide. Furthermore, optical devices for telecommunications, single- mode waveguides were fabricated using these fluorinated polyimides and copolyimides. These are both attractive and practical polymeric materials for optical applications.

